From: Chimera core
Subject: [PATCH] a quad reaching behind the eye takes the triangle's values

New3D's quad fragment shader works out four weights from the screen-space
vectors to the quad's corners. For a corner just behind the eye those
vectors are enormous, and llvmpipe, the software OpenGL the core draws
with, interpolates them into weights that are finite but wrong: the road
nearest the camera in scud's attract mode was smeared (Chimera issue 546),
after patch 0007 and the core's Mesa patch 0002 had fixed the cases where
they came out NaN or the clipper lost them. Upstream Supermodel draws the
same scene right on a graphics card. A fragment of a quad with a corner
behind the eye now takes the attributes the triangle interpolated, as
upstream does on a quad's edges.
---
--- a/Src/Graphics/New3D/R3DShaderQuads.h	2026-11-08 08:20:23.750849130 +0100
+++ b/Src/Graphics/New3D/R3DShaderQuads.h	2026-11-08 08:20:33.387474579 +0100
@@ -349,7 +349,12 @@
 	// interpolated inputs blew up - a software rasteriser gives NaN for a thin
 	// sliver (Chimera issue 469) - and such a fragment takes the attributes the
 	// triangle interpolated, as an edge does
-	bool useTri = extreme_edge;
+	// Chimera: a quad with a corner behind the eye (its 1/w negative) takes the
+	// triangle's values too. That corner's screen vectors are enormous, and
+	// llvmpipe's interpolation of them, finite but wrong, smeared the road
+	// nearest the camera in scud's attract mode (Chimera issue 546)
+	bool behind = fs_in.oneOverW[0] < 0.0 || fs_in.oneOverW[1] < 0.0 || fs_in.oneOverW[2] < 0.0 || fs_in.oneOverW[3] < 0.0;
+	bool useTri = extreme_edge || behind;
 	float u[4];
 
 	if(!useTri)
